    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 051936\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1236 PM PDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through Friday, \n  with dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Confidence is increasing in a pattern change for the weekend,\n  with a transition to cooler and gusty conditions along with\n  chances for shower activity. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nA broad high pressure ridge will remain entrenched across the\nWestern US through Friday, with well above-normal continuing. \nHighs are forecast to be 5-12 degrees above normal throughout the \nweek, with the most pronounced heat in the lower elevations of \nDeath Valley National Park where an Extreme Heat Warning is in \neffect through Friday evening. Highs there will be ~110 with \novernight lows in the 80s, resulting in major to extreme HeatRisk.\nElsewhere, heat risk will predominantly be in the minor to\nmoderate category with some small pockets of higher heat risk in \nthe Colorado River Valley. \n\nThe forecast becomes more complex over the weekend as model\nguidance attempts to resolve 1) a potentially deep trough\ndigging into the interior West, 2) a weak low well off the \nCalifornia coast, and 3) remnant moisture and vorticity from \nHurricane Rachel. How these three features interact with each\nother remains uncertain, though there is increasing support among\nmodel guidance this morning towards a deep trough digging into the\nWestern US over the weekend. This change in the weather pattern\nwould favor a trend towards cooler conditions along with\nwidespread strong wind gusts. Rainfall potential remains very\nuncertain as it will depend on if any of the remnant Rachel\nmoisture can be pulled northward towards the Mojave Desert, or if\nit is swept eastward by the deepening trough before reaching our\narea. Regardless, low chances for shower activity are advertised\nacross eastern parts of the region by Sunday and Monday as the \nweather system moves through. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Light\nwinds following typical daily directional patterns with some \nvariability are expected through the forecast period. While\nsustained speeds remain light, a sporadic easterly gust to around\n15KT cannot be ruled out during peak heating this afternoon. VFR \nconditions prevail with no operationally significant cloud cover. \nTemperatures will approach 100 in the late afternoon and early \nevening hours, but are expected to remain below triple digits. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds will remain\nlight across most of the region the next 24 hours, following \ntypical daily directional patterns with some variability. \nExceptions include KBIH in the northern Owens Valley, where up-\nvalley winds are expected to gust intermittently to 15-20KT this \nafternoon through late evening, and KDAG/the western Mojave \nDesert, where easterly gusts to around 20KT are expected through \nlate afternoon. VFR conditions will continue, with no \noperationally significant cloud cover expected. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/AVIATION...Outler\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
